# The studio IS the homepage now; old studio URLs land on / (query strings
# like ?open=ugc are forwarded automatically, so deep links keep working).
/studio.html       /             301
/studio            /             301

# Old standalone free-sample page is now folded into the single offer landing page.
/free-sample.html  /offer#claim  301
/free-sample       /offer#claim  301

# Canonical clean URL: serve the offer page at /offer, 301 the .html form to it.
# Force (!) is required because offer.html is a real file; without it Netlify
# serves the file and skips the redirect.
/offer.html        /offer        301!

# Eco ad variant: same bones as /offer, eco message match. Separate URL keeps
# /offer alive for broad traffic and isolates the eco campaign's tracking.
/offer-eco.html    /offer-eco    301!

# Investor deck: serve deck.html at the clean, shareable /deck URL.
/deck              /deck.html    200

# Secrets. netlify.toml publishes from ".", so every file in the working tree
# is a candidate for upload, and .gitignore does not enter into it: the CLI
# deploys the DIRECTORY, not the git tree. A force rule is the only thing
# standing between these paths and the open internet.
#
# This is defence in depth and not the primary control. The real fix is to stop
# publishing from the repo root, which is a bigger change than a redirect file.
# Until then, anything added to the root that holds a credential needs a line
# here on the same day it is created.
/.env                 /  301!
/.env.*               /  301!

# Internal files: never serve these publicly (real files, so force is required).
/README.md            /  301!
/ARCHITECTURE.md      /  301!
/HANDOFF.md           /  301!
/REVIEW-TEMPLATES.md  /  301!
/COMPETITORS.md       /  301!
/WINNING-ADS.md       /  301!
/HIGGSFIELD-YOUTUBE-RESEARCH.md  /  301!
/MADEBYHEXA-ASSESSMENT.md        /  301!
/MARKET-AND-FUNDING-ANALYSIS.md  /  301!
/leads/*              /  301!
/comments/*           /  301!
/transcripts/*        /  301!
/outreach/*           /  301!
/tools/*              /  301!
# Test captures. Only screenshots of pages that are already public, so this is
# tidiness rather than secrecy, but the directory is 42MB and publish = "."
# means it is a deploy-weight problem before it is anything else.
/.uitest/*            /  301!
/netlify/*            /  301!
# The research engine: prompts, scoring, the ranked corpus and generated
# reports. This is the part of the product a competitor would most like to
# read, and corpus.db is gitignored but still sits in the published directory.
/research/*           /  301!
# Database shape and migrations. Not secret on its own, but it is a map of
# every table an attacker would go looking for.
/supabase/*           /  301!
/netlify.toml         /  301!
/package.json         /  301!
/package-lock.json    /  301!
/deno.lock            /  301!

# The studio reads these two; everything else in catalog/ stays private
# (pricing margins, prompt recipes, raw engine exports). First match wins,
# so the exemptions must sit above the block.
/catalog/studio-data.json  /catalog/studio-data.json  200!
/catalog/presets.json      /catalog/presets.json      200!
# The account page's top-up chooser reads this. Generated from pricing.json but
# display only: pack sizes and dollar prices, no cost or margin fields.
/catalog/credit-packs.json /catalog/credit-packs.json 200!
# The home page's work section reads this. Captions and image paths for statics
# we published ourselves: no cost, margin or prompt data. Missing it cost the
# showcase its Statics tab on the first deploy, silently, because the block
# below answers 301 and the grid simply drew the video tabs instead.
/catalog/showcase.json     /catalog/showcase.json     200!
/catalog/*            /  301!
